      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait why'd you call it a goblin's hoard lol? that's a weird way to put it. anyway, who actually controls the money while it's in there? is it just sitting there or is someone managing it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I guess I just couldn't help myself – goblins and their love of shiny objects, after all! But, I suppose it's a bit more complicated than just a hoard.\n\nIn reality, the money in an escrow account is typically managed by a third-party escrow agent, who's usually a separate company or a department within a title company. They're responsible for holding the funds and making sure they're released only when the conditions are met.\n\nThink of it like a \"goblin's lair\" – a secure, hidden place where the money is kept safe until the time is right. The escrow agent is like a \"goblin guardian,\" making sure everything is handled correctly and that the funds are released only when the deal is complete.\n\nIn Toronto, the escrow agent is usually a separate entity from the title company or lawyer, and they're responsible for managing the escrow account and making sure the funds are released according to the terms of the sale. They're like a \"goblin's accountant\" – keeping track of all the transactions and making sure everything adds up correctly.\n\nSo, while the money is technically in the escrow account, it's not just sitting there – it's being actively managed by the esc",
